  "source": "https://arxiv.org/html/2309.15703 (v3, Appendix B.5, C.3; accessed 2026-10-12)",
  "conclusion": "Cracker mismatch exists in mocap and quadratic fits too, so RGB tracking alone cannot explain it. Maximum surface-normal tilt is 1.798 degrees (yaw alone is not tipping). The source Appendix C.3 explicitly describes uneven cardboard contact and anisotropic friction; the measured mismatch is consistent with the constant-friction model limitation, not a demonstrated clock error. Appendix B.5 reports more variation of cracker tilt-test values. A shared bias is not detected by fitter/baseline agreement or small scatter. Source confirms synchronised/downsampled 30 Hz; no truth or clock correction is applied. This does not independently isolate the physical contact cause in the original lab.",
  "single_push_interval": "One push contributes no between-push bootstrap variation; measurement assumptions alone make its narrow interval. Such intervals must be unavailable, not interpreted as confidence."
}
